Bridge to the next phase

RFC-0014 intentionally serves as the bridge from theory to implementation. The next documents should no longer introduce new cognitive concepts. Instead, they should specify how this architecture is realized.

That implementation phase naturally begins with:

MOS Storage Specification — translating Memory Spaces into PostgreSQL schemas, tables, indexes, partitions, graph structures, vector storage, and lifecycle rules.
MOS API Specification — defining the stable interfaces (remember(), recall(), reflect(), plan(), search(), etc.) that any agent framework can use.
MOS Worker Specification — defining the contracts, queues, state transitions, and responsibilities for every background worker.
MOS Network Specification — defining distributed synchronization, federation, replication, and cross-node cognition.

At this point, MOS has a conceptual foundation comparable to what an instruction set architecture is for a CPU: a stable contract that multiple implementations can build upon while remaining interoperable.
